---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-home.avif";
import assessmentIcon from "../assets/icons/assessment.svg";
import lecturesIcon from "../assets/icons/lectures.svg";
import peopleIcon from "../assets/icons/people.svg";
import sessionsIcon from "../assets/icons/sessions.svg";
import timetableIcon from "../assets/icons/timetable.svg";
import CourseStats from "../components/CourseStats.astro";
import LittlesLaw from "../components/LittlesLaw.astro";
import Photo from "../components/Photo.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { navGroups, sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import NavGroups from "../components/NavGroups.astro";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code} ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A single queue folded into five rows of gold blocks on a dark ground, each block one person, the line ending at one open counter with one server"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Every queue you have stood in was designed by somebody, and almost none of
    them told you how. You pick one in week 1 and keep it until week 12.
  </p>

  <CourseStats />

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    In week 1 you pick a queue. A till, a counter, a platform, a help desk,
    something you can physically stand next to twice a week. It is yours for
    the semester.
  </p>

  <Photo
    name="shopfront-queue"
    caption="Somebody chose how this works. How many serve at once, in what order, and what happens to the person who arrives at the wrong moment. By week 5 you can read all of it off the pavement."
  />

  <p>
    You measure it by hand, with a pen, badly at first. You work out its
    arrival rate and its service distribution and how much of the wait is the
    tax you pay for variability. You record every time somebody is served out
    of order and what it cost the people behind them. You write down what it
    would take to change it, predict the effect before anybody tells you, and
    then say out loud who waits longer after your change, because somebody
    always does.
  </p>
  <p>
    In week 12 you defend that change in front of the room, and if you can
    persuade them, in front of whoever runs the queue.
  </p>

  <LittlesLaw />

  <h2>What the course thinks</h2>
  <p>
    That the arithmetic is the easy half. There are four pieces of it here,
    and they are enough to embarrass most people who design queues
    professionally. Little's Law will tell you how long a line is waiting
    without you timing anybody in it. Kingman will tell you why the counter got
    worse after the efficiency drive.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="pull">
    <p>
      Spare capacity is not waste. It is what you are bought instead of a
      queue.
    </p>
    <footer>Week 3, variability is the tax</footer>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    The hard half is the question none of the equations answer. A queue
    discipline is a ranking of people, and nearly every one you meet was set
    without saying so. Emergency triage is the honourable exception, which is
    why we spend a week on it, and why we spend the week after that on the
    queues that publish nothing at all.
  </p>

  <h2>What people said afterwards</h2>
  <div class="voices">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        I came in wanting to prove the bus interchange was badly run. It is not.
        It is running at ninety-four percent and nobody has told the people who
        set the timetable what that costs.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, environments and design</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The redesign studio in week 11 was brutal and it saved my mark. Three
        people found the group my change would have hurt in about four minutes.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, public policy</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Genuinely cannot stand in a queue normally any more. My family have
        asked me to stop.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, computer science</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Third-year students in any degree. You need to be comfortable with
    algebra, averages and a spreadsheet, and that is genuinely the whole
    prerequisite; there is no calculus and no proof in the assessment. You
    also need to be willing to stand outside a shop for an hour with a
    clipboard, in the cold, being looked at.
  </p>
  <p>
    It suits people heading for operations, service design, health
    administration, transport, systems performance and public policy. It has
    also been taken by a lot of people who were simply annoyed about something
    and wanted to know whether they were right.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is not a queueing theory course. A queueing theory course would spend
    the semester on Markov chains and never send you outside. Four results
    appear here, each one because you need it to say something about a real
    line, and the exam that would test them does not exist.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card
      title="Timetable"
      href="/timetable/"
      image={timetableIcon}
      imageAlt="A grid of twelve weeks, one of them outlined"
    >
      <p>The whole semester on one page, and the two busy stretches marked.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Lectures"
      href="/lectures/"
      image={lecturesIcon}
      imageAlt="A board with three lines of writing, faced by a row of people"
    >
      <p>The twelve weeks, and the claim each one makes.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title={sessionLabels.plural}
      href="/sessions/"
      image={sessionsIcon}
      imageAlt="A clipboard with two struck tally groups and a third in progress"
    >
      <p>Two hours a week, mostly outdoors, mostly with a stopwatch.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Assessment"
      href="/assessments/"
      image={assessmentIcon}
      imageAlt="Four bars of different heights standing on a rule"
    >
      <p>Four pieces, all of them about the same queue.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="People"
      href="/people/"
      image={peopleIcon}
      imageAlt="Four figures side by side, the last one drawn as an outline"
    >
      <p>Who teaches it, which weeks, and when they are free.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <p class="footnote">
    Needing more time is ordinary and asking early is free.
    <a href={withBase("/extensions/")}>Ask for an extension</a>, or read the
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies</a> before you need them.
  </p>

  <NavGroups groups={navGroups} />
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The opening paragraph carries the whole argument, so it gets the weight
     the headings would otherwise take. One size step, no second colour. */
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }

  .pull {
    margin-block: 1.75rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
  }

  .pull p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .pull footer,
  .voices footer {
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .voices {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-block: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .voices blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .voices blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .footnote {
    margin-block-start: 2rem;
    padding-block-start: 1.25rem;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  @media (width < 60rem) {
    .voices {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  @media (width < 44rem) {
    .lede {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    .pull p {
      font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
